I was sitting on the edge of the boat my  back against the water mask check fins  check air pressure 3000 psi I grabbed my  camera and started the countdown three  two one  I rolled back and hit the water as the  bubble settled and the visibility  cleared I felt it  this could be the dive I have always  dreamed of as I descended I was  surrounded by over 70 sharks from  different species we reached the sandy  floor and from the semicircle my mom was  to my left I started shooting away my  favorite a beautiful lemon shark arrived  finally twenty minutes into the dive the  thing I've been waiting for two massive  tiger sharks one of them measuring over  15 feet in length  they started circling the group and  getting close very close as the larger  of the two sharks swim past me heading  towards my mom I saw its eyes turn white  something they do right before biting  the shark suddenly turned and smacked me  with its tail I lost my balance but as  soon as I regained it I click the  shutter and saw the scariest image I've  ever taken the shark took a bite at my  mom luckily she have her camera with her  and was able to use it as a shield but  the shark kept biting pushing her  further and further away from the group  with each bite  finally it let go  just when I thought we were safe the  shark turned and this time headed  straight at me the same thing happened  it's I turned white and a bite followed  after this we decided to abort the dive  once we reach the surface and the fear  and adrenaline invaded my head flooded  with questions why did the dive go so  wrong who was at fault in hindsight we  changed the behavior of the shark by  chumming the water which is putting that  bait in a cage to lure them in the shark  was acting normal  it was looking for the food it was  sensing in fact it was even being gentle  remember this animals weigh 1,500 pounds  have teeth as sharp as razors that can  crush a turtle's shell just like that  yet none of us had a scratch not my mom  not me not even the crystal dome of the  camera had a scratch this dive was a  turning point for me for the past 10  years I've been traveling the most  remote places in the world looking for  animals that we've driven to the brink  of extinction all to promote  conservation through photography my  thinking was that if I show you the  giant of the ocean some of its tiniest  creatures there's a seahorse in there  let's see if you can spot it or even  pixels most famous stars  your mind will be flooded with curiosity  and you want to experience and explore  the ocean and I know deep inside that  once you do you'll fall in love with it  and want to protect it just like I did  however I was not having the impact I  hoped for as much as I wanted my passion  and love for the ocean to be contagious  the truth is we are very detached from  the ocean and it is very hard to protect  something that seems so foreign for  example on the Left we have a picture of  my Labradoodle smokey this handsome dog  got a hundred and seven likes on  Instagram on the right we have a flying  whale with only forty four likes I mean  smoke is very cute and all but two and a  half times as cute as a flying whale  moreover after my encounter with the  shark I finally understood that there's  no image beautiful enough or a story  compelling enough that I can tell you  that that I can tell someone who's  afraid of the ocean or afraid of sharks  that will make them want to dive I fear  I could now relate to if I couldn't  reach people's hearts I needed to find a  better way I came across this  organization called Oceana whose mission  is save the ocean feed the world I  realized that the ocean is much more  than a beautiful place today 1 billion  people depend on the ocean including  some of the world's poorest but we're  taking much more than the ocean can  produce and in the process with the  destroying a very delicate balance that  took millions of years to perfect to add  to the problem we will soon reach 9  billion people and we don't have the  resources to fill such a large and  growing population  the ocean offers an abundant and  sustainable source of food for the world  and on like land-based agriculture or  farming wild fish produce no greenhouse  gases does not consume our precious and  scarce freshwater and does not utilize  fertile land but to take advantage of  this natural resource we need to change  the nature of our relationship with the  ocean sharks are a sign of a healthy  ocean because they protect the coral  reefs by controlling the population of  smaller predatory fish who feed on  herbivores without these herbivores  algae expands and coral can no longer  compete killing of the reef these reefs  are critical they are the breeding  ground for all fish species without them  fish populations plate today we kill a  hundred million sharks per year that is  almost twelve thousand sharks per hour  imagine the impact we are having on the  balance of marine ecosystems we have  already wiped out over 90% of all large  fish species like tunas and sharks  in contrast sharks killed 12 humans per  year so I ask you who is the deadliest  predator who should we be afraid of for  some people  images like the ones I've shown you  awaken our sense of wonder and make us  want to explore what 70% of the world's  surface is made of for others not so  much but no matter what group you're in  the ocean is our most precious and  valuable resource and it holds a key to  our future so what can we do to protect  it we can follow and support  organizations like Oceana  we can elect government officials who  support corrosion policy and we can eat  sustainable food it is not too late we  can replenish fish populations in as  little as 10 years making this goal  attainable in our lifetime and easier to  push in the political  agenda let's change how we coexist with  the ocean and together let's provide 1  billion healthy and sustainable fish  meals a day to the world thank you  [Applause]  [Music]
 

